{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0cfad3f747","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-350","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dunmore National Park is in northern Japan. It was established in 1959.</li><li>Its area is 8,377 square kilometers.</li><li>Redfern National Park is in southern Norway. It was established in 1952.</li><li>Its area is 3,713 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks known for their old-growth forests.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two parks were established.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Dunmore National Park and Redfern National Park are parks known for their old-growth forests.","Dunmore National Park was established in 1959, while Redfern National Park was established in 1952.","Dunmore National Park is in northern Japan, and Redfern National Park is in southern Norway.","The area of Dunmore National Park (8,377 square kilometers) is greater than that of Redfern National Park (3,713 square kilometers)."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b08abbea41","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-352","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Ironwood Tower is in western Kenya. It was completed in 1965.</li><li>Its height is 402 meters.</li><li>The Juniper Tower is in southern Norway. It was completed in 1997.</li><li>Its height is 267 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two towers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Ironwood Tower, which is in western Kenya, is one of several towers built mainly of steel.","The Ironwood Tower was completed in 1965, while the Juniper Tower was completed in 1997.","The Ironwood Tower is in western Kenya, and the Juniper Tower is in southern Norway.","The height of the Ironwood Tower (402 meters) is greater than that of the Juniper Tower (267 meters)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b078f80627","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-355","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Fenwick is in central Spain. It was first surveyed in 1982.</li><li>Its surface area is 822 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Merrow is in northern Japan. It was first surveyed in 1977.</li><li>Its surface area is 262 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Lake Fenwick and Lake Merrow.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Fenwick (822 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Merrow (262 square kilometers).","Lake Fenwick is in central Spain, and Lake Merrow is in northern Japan.","Both Lake Fenwick and Lake Merrow are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Fenwick was first surveyed in 1982, while Lake Merrow was first surveyed in 1977."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.bd799e7b5f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-364","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Pembrook is in eastern Australia. It was first climbed in 1896.</li><li>Its height is 4,855 meters.</li><li>Mount Glenrock is in eastern Poland. It was first climbed in 2017.</li><li>Its height is 2,124 meters.</li><li>Both are dormant volcanoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two mountains were first climbed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of Mount Pembrook (4,855 meters) is greater than that of Mount Glenrock (2,124 meters).","Both Mount Pembrook and Mount Glenrock are dormant volcanoes.","Mount Pembrook was first climbed in 1896, while Mount Glenrock was first climbed in 2017.","Mount Pembrook is in eastern Australia, and Mount Glenrock is in eastern Poland."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c0613ef717","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-365","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Sable Tower is in western Kenya. It was completed in 2019.</li><li>Its height is 431 meters.</li><li>The Pembrook Tower is in central Spain. It was completed in 2003.</li><li>Its height is 382 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two towers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Sable Tower is in western Kenya, and the Pembrook Tower is in central Spain.","The Sable Tower, which is in western Kenya, is one of several observation towers open to the public.","The height of the Sable Tower (431 meters) is greater than that of the Pembrook Tower (382 meters).","The Sable Tower was completed in 2019, while the Pembrook Tower was completed in 2003."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8b352538e1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-375","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Thornbury is in central Canada. It was first surveyed in 1936.</li><li>Its surface area is 636 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Wexford is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 1985.</li><li>Its surface area is 500 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Lake Thornbury and Lake Wexford.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Thornbury is in central Canada, and Lake Wexford is in northern India.","The surface area of Lake Thornbury (636 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Wexford (500 square kilometers).","Both Lake Thornbury and Lake Wexford are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","Lake Thornbury was first surveyed in 1936, while Lake Wexford was first surveyed in 1985."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7aebd647a3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-380","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Orla Observatory is in northern India. It began operating in 1966.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 10 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Observatory is in eastern Australia. It began operating in 2010.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 7 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two observatorys began operating.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Orla Observatory began operating in 1966, while the Cresthill Observatory began operating in 2010.","The Orla Observatory is in northern India, and the Cresthill Observatory is in eastern Australia.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Orla Observatory (10 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Observatory (7 meters).","Both the Orla Observatory and the Cresthill Observatory are radio observatories."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8b3da01d9d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-382","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Alder Tower is in southern New Zealand. It was completed in 1890.</li><li>Its height is 388 meters.</li><li>The Wexford Tower is in western Scotland. It was completed in 1975.</li><li>Its height is 175 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Alder Tower and the Wexford Tower.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of the Alder Tower (388 meters) is greater than that of the Wexford Tower (175 meters).","Both the Alder Tower and the Wexford Tower are observation towers open to the public.","The Alder Tower was completed in 1890, while the Wexford Tower was completed in 1975.","The Alder Tower is in southern New Zealand, and the Wexford Tower is in western Scotland."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b37ab008f6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-382","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Alder Tower is in southern New Zealand. It was completed in 1890.</li><li>Its height is 388 meters.</li><li>The Wexford Tower is in western Scotland. It was completed in 1975.</li><li>Its height is 175 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two towers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Alder Tower was completed in 1890, while the Wexford Tower was completed in 1975.","The height of the Alder Tower (388 meters) is greater than that of the Wexford Tower (175 meters).","The Alder Tower is in southern New Zealand, and the Wexford Tower is in western Scotland.","The Alder Tower, which is in southern New Zealand, is one of several observation towers open to the public."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2b1a1c146e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-385","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Wexford Tower is in central Canada. It was completed in 1928.</li><li>Its height is 348 meters.</li><li>The Northam Tower is in northern India. It was completed in 1925.</li><li>Its height is 195 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two towers differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of the Wexford Tower (348 meters) is greater than that of the Northam Tower (195 meters).","Both the Wexford Tower and the Northam Tower are towers built mainly of steel.","The Wexford Tower was completed in 1928, while the Northam Tower was completed in 1925.","The Wexford Tower, which is in central Canada, is one of several towers built mainly of steel."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both towers. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tower."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7dacf57799","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-385","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Wexford Tower is in central Canada. It was completed in 1928.</li><li>Its height is 348 meters.</li><li>The Northam Tower is in northern India. It was completed in 1925.</li><li>Its height is 195 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two towers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Wexford Tower was completed in 1928, while the Northam Tower was completed in 1925.","The height of the Wexford Tower (348 meters) is greater than that of the Northam Tower (195 meters).","The Wexford Tower is in central Canada, and the Northam Tower is in northern India.","The Wexford Tower, which is in central Canada, is one of several towers built mainly of steel."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.591ab6e1ab","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-387","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Alder Museum is in eastern Australia. It opened in 1919.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 652,421 visitors.</li><li>The Quarry Museum is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 1964.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 602,601 visitors.</li><li>Both are art museums with free admission.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two museums.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Alder Museum, which is in eastern Australia, is one of several art museums with free admission.","The Alder Museum opened in 1919, while the Quarry Museum opened in 1964.","The annual attendance of the Alder Museum (652,421 visitors) is greater than that of the Quarry Museum (602,601 visitors).","The Alder Museum is in eastern Australia, and the Quarry Museum is in southern New Zealand."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c8d0cc207c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-396","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Sable is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1904.</li><li>Its surface area is 663 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Valewood is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 2007.</li><li>Its surface area is 313 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two lakes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Sable was first surveyed in 1904, while Lake Valewood was first surveyed in 2007.","Lake Sable is in southern Norway, and Lake Valewood is in northern India.","Lake Sable, which is in southern Norway, is one of several reservoirs created by dams.","The surface area of Lake Sable (663 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Valewood (313 square kilometers)."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0110f865dd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-16","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Haddad is a psychologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Haddad had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Haddad found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Haddad concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Haddad found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","Haddad concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","To investigate the question, Haddad had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Psychologist Felipe Haddad set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7cdbe7e18d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-22","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Takeda is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Takeda grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Takeda found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Takeda concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.","Takeda concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","To investigate the question, Takeda grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.","Agricultural scientist Priya Takeda set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d7b43daa60","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-34","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Takeda is an entomologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Takeda set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Takeda found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Takeda concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.","Takeda found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.","To investigate the question, Takeda set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.","Entomologist Tomás Takeda set out to learn whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.910de4a414","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-46","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Okafor is a geologist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Okafor measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Okafor found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Okafor concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","To investigate the question, Okafor measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.","Geologist Kenji Okafor set out to learn whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.","Okafor concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d322021574","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-48","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dmitri Varga is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Varga asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Varga found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Varga concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.","To investigate the question, Varga asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Varga concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","Nutrition researcher Dmitri Varga set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.502a930f97","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-50","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Delacroix is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Delacroix asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Delacroix found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Delacroix asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Delacroix concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","Delacroix found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.","Nutrition researcher Ayumi Delacroix set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.264a1db4dd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-50","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Delacroix is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Delacroix asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Delacroix found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Delacroix concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","Delacroix found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.","Nutrition researcher Ayumi Delacroix set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.","To investigate the question, Delacroix asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.48721d4b87","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-63","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Maren Brennan is an astronomer.</li><li>Brennan wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Brennan measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Brennan found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Brennan concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Brennan measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Because the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days, Brennan concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","Brennan concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","Brennan found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.640de513a5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-68","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Moreau is a linguist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Moreau gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Moreau found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Moreau concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors, Moreau concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","To investigate the question, Moreau gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.","Moreau found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.","Moreau concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b7993f90be","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-78","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Abara is a botanist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.</li><li>Abara grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.</li><li>Abara found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.</li><li>Abara concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Botanist Ayumi Abara set out to learn whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.","To investigate the question, Abara grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.","Abara found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.","Abara concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.6cb4b3c612","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-88","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Moreau is an urban planner.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.</li><li>Moreau measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.</li><li>Moreau found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.</li><li>Moreau concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Moreau measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.","Urban planner Elias Moreau set out to learn whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.","Moreau concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","Moreau found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c671d60e52","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-90","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Varga is a hydrologist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Varga compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Varga found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Varga concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Hydrologist Amara Varga set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.","Varga found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.","Varga concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.","To investigate the question, Varga compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ba37b0648c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-104","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Jonas Duarte is a chemist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Duarte placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Duarte found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Duarte concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Duarte concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.","To investigate the question, Duarte placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.","Duarte found that most samples broke apart within four months.","Because most samples broke apart within four months, Duarte concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a4eead2d0e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-107","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Halvorsen is a chemist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Halvorsen placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Halvorsen found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Chemist Diego Halvorsen set out to learn whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.","Halvorsen found that most samples broke apart within four months.","Halvorsen concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.fddd8b69a6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-114","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Mensah is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Mensah asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Mensah found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Mensah concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Mensah asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.","Mensah found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.","Mensah concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.","Sleep researcher Beatriz Mensah set out to learn whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b1d8489583","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-124","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Quintero is an ornithologist.</li><li>Quintero wanted to find out whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.</li><li>Quintero measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.</li><li>Quintero found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.</li><li>Quintero concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Quintero concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.","Quintero found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.","Ornithologist Ayumi Quintero set out to learn whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.","To investigate the question, Quintero measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c66e77eaeb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-124","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Quintero is an ornithologist.</li><li>Quintero wanted to find out whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.</li><li>Quintero measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.</li><li>Quintero found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.</li><li>Quintero concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings, Quintero concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.","To investigate the question, Quintero measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.","Quintero found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.","Quintero concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.78dec750b5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-128","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Moreau is a public health researcher.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Moreau counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Moreau found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Moreau concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Moreau concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.","Public health researcher Zara Moreau set out to learn whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.","Moreau found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.","To investigate the question, Moreau counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.36c476df0c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-134","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Duarte is an archaeologist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Duarte analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Duarte found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Duarte concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Duarte found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","Duarte concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","Because many pots were made from clay found only far inland, Duarte concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","To investigate the question, Duarte analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b00b1e92e1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-143","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Castellano is a neuroscientist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.</li><li>Castellano scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.</li><li>Castellano found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.</li><li>Castellano concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Castellano concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.","Castellano found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.","Neuroscientist Tomás Castellano set out to learn whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.","To investigate the question, Castellano scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.23aa6a9b1c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-146","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Hana Iyer is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Iyer fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Iyer found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Iyer concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Marine ecologist Hana Iyer set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","To investigate the question, Iyer fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Iyer concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","Iyer found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0b1174502a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-149","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Kowalski is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Kowalski fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Kowalski found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Marine ecologist Sofia Kowalski set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","Kowalski concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","To investigate the question, Kowalski fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Kowalski found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.40414f1ee7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-150","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Varga is a climate scientist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Varga compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Varga found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Varga concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","To investigate the question, Varga compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Varga concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Climate scientist Chen Varga set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9c270f583b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-152","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Abara is a climate scientist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Abara compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Abara found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Abara concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Abara compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Abara found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","Climate scientist Kofi Abara set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.","Abara concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.258abc05ce","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-160","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Jonas Asante is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Asante moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Asante found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Asante concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Asante concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.","To investigate the question, Asante moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.","Asante found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","Behavioral economist Jonas Asante set out to learn whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.eee4da7661","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-177","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Vance is a music researcher.</li><li>Vance wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Vance tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Vance found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Vance concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Vance concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","To investigate the question, Vance tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Music researcher Zara Vance set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.","Vance found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."}
